    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Babylon was a small town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, who ruled from 1792 to 1750 BCE, made it the capital of a kingdom that covered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</li><li>It was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> in 689 BCE and rebuilt, became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> from 626 to 539 BCE, and then served as a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it declined under Parthian, Sasanian and Muslim rule, and the last known habitation, a small village, dates from the 11th century CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Babylon has been estimated to have been the largest city in the world c. 1770 to c. 1670 BCE and again c. 612 to c. 320 BCE, and perhaps the first city to reach a population above 200,000.</p></aside>
    </section>

