    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">2217 to 2193 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A year name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a> records the founding of temples in Babylon, its earliest reasonably firm mention</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1792 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> becomes king and builds Babylon into the capital of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1595 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The city is thought to have been sacked by the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">689 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> destroys the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> takes Babylon and ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">331 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> enters Babylon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1899</span><span class="rf-text">Robert Koldewey begins the first scientific excavations</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2019</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribes Babylon as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri.jpg" alt="Shar-Kali-Sharri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Impression_of_an_Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_The_Divine_Sharkalisharri_Prince_of_Akkad_Ibni-Sharrum_the_Scribe_his_servant.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt 2011, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babylon stood on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, in what is now the area of Hillah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The name appears in Akkadian as <i>Bābilim</i>, "gate of the god(s)", but this reading is increasingly viewed as a Semitic folk etymology for an unknown original name, which I. J. Gelb thought was Babilla. The earliest reasonably firm mention is one of the year names of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a> (2217 to 2193 BCE), which records the founding of temples of Annunitum and Ilaba in the city. Babylon was then a religious and cultural centre but a town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, not an independent state, and it was ruled by a governor, an <i><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">ensi</a></i>. It was later included in the realm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, whose records name an <i>ensi</i> there.</p><p>Classical writers dated the founding much earlier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, placed it in 2286 BCE under a first king, Belus; Berossus, according to Pliny, implies 2243 BCE; and Stephanus of Byzantium implies 2231 BCE. All fall in the 23rd century BCE, but no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> records correspond to them. The Weidner Chronicle says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> built Babylon "in front of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>", though Van de Mieroop suggested that the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> is meant. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Genesis 10:10 says that Babel was founded by Nimrod with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and Akkad. A fragmentary votive inscription of about 2700 BCE has been proposed to name Babylon, but the attribution is considered doubtful.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babylon&#8221; cites where it covers Names:</p><ul><li>Ferris J. Stephens, Votive and Historical Texts from Babylonia and Assyria (Yale University Press, 1937)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babylon was at first a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> with little territory, overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> (r. 1792 to 1750 BCE) it became the capital of a kingdom that took in the cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and from his time the region was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. Babylon supplanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and Eridu as the main religious centre of the south. It has been estimated that Babylon was the largest city in the world c. 1770 to c. 1670 BCE, and again c. 612 to c. 320 BCE, and perhaps the first to exceed 200,000 people. The whole site covers about 1,000 hectares, with about 450 hectares inside the walls.</p><p>The greatest rebuilding came after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> freed Babylon from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and the city became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> ordered the reconstruction of the imperial grounds, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a>, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a>, the most prominent of eight gates. Before his reign construction was mainly of unbaked brick; his reign saw heavy use of baked brick. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens</a>, said to have been built for his homesick wife Amytis, were counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>, but they are not mentioned in any surviving Babylonian text. Robert Koldewey thought he had found their foundations, many historians disagree about the location, and Stephanie Dalley has argued that they stood near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebuchadnezzar_II_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to a Babylonian king list, Amorite rule began in the 19th or 18th century BCE with Sumu-abum, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el">Sumu-la-El</a> is usually given as the first of the First Babylonian dynasty. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> the empire weakened under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>. The city is thought to have been sacked by the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a> in 1595 BCE, in the reign of Samsu-Ditana, the last ruler of the dynasty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> then took power, renamed the city Karduniash and held it for 435 years, until 1160 BCE; it later paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and fell under Assyrian and Elamite pressure. From 911 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> was under constant Assyrian domination or direct control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> escaped Assyrian rule and, with his allies, destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> between 626 and 609 BCE, so that Babylon became the capital of the Neo-Babylonian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>.</p><p>In 539 BCE the empire fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Babylon became the capital of the 9th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Native Babylonian kings briefly regained independence in 522, 521 and 482 BCE, but these revolts were put down. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> entered in October 331 BCE after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at Gaugamela, and died in 323 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>'s palace. Babylon then lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and later belonged to the Parthian and Sasanian empires for nine centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> took it briefly in 116 CE, but Hadrian gave up the conquests east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. In the mid 7th century the Muslim empire took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and dissolved Babylon as a province.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Babylon&#8221; cites where it covers Neo-Babylonian Empire, Renewed Persian rule, Muslim conquest, Modern era:</p><ul><li>Karen Radner, A Short History of Babylon (Bloomsbury Academic, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy.jpg" alt="Babylonian astronomy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylonian_tablet_recording_Halley%27s_comet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Linguica at English Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Babylon was the main cultural and political centre of the Akkadian-speaking region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, which has shifted slightly since ancient times, ran through the city, and its mounds mark the main buildings: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a>, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> with shrines of Ea and Nabu, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a> on the Kasr mound. Old Babylonian texts often treat the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> as a supreme deity and Marduk as his son; Marduk was later raised to a higher status. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a> and mathematics were revitalised and scholars completed maps of constellations, though by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> over-taxation and war had led to a decay of the main shrines and canals.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described a moat, an enormously tall and broad wall cemented with bitumen and a hundred gates, and customs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>. After Archibald Sayce's pronouncement in 1883 his account was largely taken as Greek folklore, though Dalley and others have recently suggested taking it seriously. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Cleitarchus also described the city, with variable accuracy. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple and exiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to Babylon, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-chronicles">Babylonian Chronicles</a> record the defeat. Under Parthian and Sasanian rule the people spoke varieties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and still called their land Babylon; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a>, the Mandaean religion, the East Syrian rite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> come from this culture, and Babylon was the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sennacherib.jpg" alt="Sennacherib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanherib-tr-4271.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Timo Roller, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, facing continual revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, destroyed the city completely in 689 BCE: its walls, temples and palaces were razed and the rubble was thrown into the Arakhtu. The destruction shocked many, and his murder by two of his sons was considered an act of atonement. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> rebuilt the city. In 652 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin">Shamash-shum-ukin</a> started a civil war against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, and Babylon was besieged and starved into surrender. In 539 BCE, according to the account elaborated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> diverted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> during a feast and entered along the river bed while most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in the centre did not know of the breach. Alexander is said to have demolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a> in 331 BCE, and most of its remains were heaped on the Homera mound.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Babylon declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> in the 10th century and al-Qazwini in the 13th describe a small village, and medieval Arabic writings say that bricks were taken from the site to build cities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. Large-scale mining of baked bricks, mostly from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, continued in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> 13:19 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> 50 say that Babylon would never be inhabited, but coins from the Parthian, Sasanian and Arabic periods show continuous settlement. The last known habitation dates from the 11th century CE, when the place was called the "small village of Babel".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate.jpg" alt="Ishtar Gate" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berl%C3%ADn,_Museo_de_P%C3%A9rgamo_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LBM1948, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For centuries European travellers could not find the city, and some took Fallujah for it, while others called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> Babylon or New Babylon. Claudius Rich excavated in 1811 to 1812 and 1817, and Fulgence Fresnel, Julius Oppert and Felix Thomas in 1852 to 1854; much of their work was lost when a transport ship and rafts sank on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in May 1855. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> dug for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> from 1879 to 1882, and his methods damaged the archaeological context. Robert Koldewey's German team made the first scientific excavations, daily from 1899 to 1917, concentrating on the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/processional-way">Processional Way</a> and the city wall, and pieces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where it was reconstructed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> Museum. Most tablets from modern excavations remain unpublished.</p><p>In 1978 the Ba'athist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> began the Archaeological Restoration of Babylon Project, rebuilding features such as the Southern Palace on the ruins, with bricks bearing his name. After the invasion of 2003 US forces built Camp Alpha on the ruins, and John Curtis of the British Museum reported damage to the Ishtar Gate, crushed brick pavements, and trenches cut into ancient deposits. The site was handed to the Iraqi Ministry of Culture in 2005, reopened to visitors in 2009 and inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2019. Villages lie within the ancient outer walls, and building is encroaching on the ruins.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/enuma-elis">Enūma Eliš<span>c. 1100 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137<span>c. 570 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)<span>1842</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Babylon was a city on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> that rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and again under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, and served later empires as a regional capital. It declined from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> and was a village by the 11th century CE. Its ruins near Hillah are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

